# Debugging and inspection

## Prefer print-debugging

Insert probes, run, remove. Faster than stepping debuggers for most Forth work.

```forth
require debugs.fs
: foo ( n -- )
  ~~              \ file:line + .s (Emacs compilation-mode friendly)
  dup 0< if ~~bt then ;
```

`~~bt` / `~~1bt` — stack + backtrace; `once` — run probe body only once. After `marker`, `~~` may show `*somewhere*` or wrong filename — do not rely on line numbers past markers.

## Stack and memory inspection

```forth
.s f.s depth fdepth
? addr          \ one cell at addr
dump            \ memory region
see myword      \ decompile (comments lost)
xt-see ' myword
```

**`.r` is pictured numeric output**, not return-stack display. `maxdepth-.s` caps items shown (default 9).

## Other tools

```forth
\ gforth depth-changes.fs my-file.fs   \ stray stack items at blank lines
+ltrace myword   \ line trace (debugs.fs)
'replace-word    \ hot-patch: replace-word xt1 xt2 (experiments only)
```

Assertions — see `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc`. Error message format and backtraces — same file, § ch.6.

Use **`gforth`** while debugging; **`gforth-fast`** gives weaker backtraces on primitive faults.
